  <content>
  <section><title> Activity </title>

<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.geogebra.org/material/iframe/id/nB8CUK3Y/width/678/height/483/border/888888/sfsb/true/smb/false/stb/false/stbh/false/ai/false/asb/false/sri/true/rc/false/ld/false/sdz/false/ctl/false"/></media><para>In the Tower of Hanoi puzzle, a set of discs sits on a peg, while there are 2 other empty pegs.</para>
<para>A move in the Tower of Hanoi puzzle involves taking a disc and moving it to another peg. There are two rules:</para>
<list>
  <item> Only move 1 disc at a time. </item>
  <item> Never put a larger disc on top of a smaller one. </item>
</list>
<para>You complete the puzzle by building the complete tower on a different peg from the starting peg.</para>


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the smallest number of moves in which you are able to complete the puzzle with 3 discs?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>7 moves</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the smallest number of moves in which you are able to complete the puzzle with 4 discs?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>15 moves</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Jada says she used the solution for 3 discs to help her solve the puzzle for 4 discs. Describe how this might happen.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Jada moved the tower of discs 1 – 3, then moved disc 4, and then moved the remaining discs 1 – 3 onto it.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many moves do you think it will take to complete a puzzle with 5 discs? Explain.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>31 moves. For example: Double the previous answer, and then add 1. The number of moves is 31 because \(2\cdot15+1=31\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many moves do you think it will take to complete a puzzle with 7 discs? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>127 moves</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>In this activity, you have started to find a <term class="glossary-tooltip">sequence</term> of <term class="glossary-tooltip">terms</term>.</para>
<para>Write these definitions in your math notebook.</para>
<list>
  <item>A sequence is a list of numbers, possibly going on forever, such as all the odd positive integers arranged in order: 1, 3, 5, 7, . . . .</item>
  <item>The term (of a sequence) is one of the numbers in a sequence.</item>
</list></section>
